    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011257": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty Position in RNA Biology and Cancer- Department of Cell Biology and Physiology, WashU School of Medicine",
      "description": "Department Cell Biology &amp; Physiology Division Cell Biology And Physiology I &amp; R Track Tenure Rank Assistant Professor Title Tenure-Track Faculty Position in RNA Biology and Cancer- Department of Cell Biology and Physiology, WashU School of Medicine Position Description The Department of Cell Biology and Physiology (CBP) at WashU School of Medicine invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level. &nbsp; We seek an outstanding investigator who will establish an innovative, externally funded research program, with a particular focus on the cell biology of RNA and cancer. &nbsp; We encourage applications from investigators who integrate fundamental RNA biology with important questions in human disease, who address fundamental questions in cancer cell biology and/or who develop new quantitative ways to interrogate RNA biology at molecular, cellular, and organismal scales. &nbsp; The successful candidate will be expected to establish a successful research program, contribute to the intellectual and educational mission of the Department, mentor graduate students and postdoctoral fellows, and lead service and teaching activities appropriate to their career stage. &nbsp; The Department of Cell Biology and Physiology is an interdisciplinary and collaborative research community with programs spanning quantitative cell biology, cancer biology, cell signaling, membrane biology, cytoskeleton, organelle biology, metabolism, genomics, proteomics, and structural approaches. &nbsp; The department is embedded within the broad and deep research environment of WashU School of Medicine and other WashU schools, which provides extensive opportunities for interactions across departments, institutes, and research programs. &nbsp; WashU School of Medicine offers exceptional resources for investigators working at the interface of basic biology and human disease, including advanced genomics, single-cell and spatial technologies, quantitative imaging, structural biology, computational biology, and translational research infrastructure. &nbsp; The successful candidate will be able to develop collaborations not only within basic sciences, but also with translational and clinical research. Basic Qualifications Applicants must hold a PhD, MD, MD/PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree and have demonstrated excellence in research. &nbsp; Special Instructions Please include the following materials: A cover letter describing interest in and fit with our department and institution A curriculum vitae A research statement of accomplishments and research directions (4 pages max) A statement of teaching and mentoring experience and philosophy (1 page) Names and contact information for three references. Applications will be reviewed beginning November 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. &nbsp; Contact Name Rhiannon Amato Contact Email amator@wustl.edu Posting Date 10/13/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
