    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011254": {
      "title": "Chief of Research, Division of General Internal Medicine",
      "description": "Department Department of Medicine Division General Internal Medicine Track Research Rank Associate Professor/Full Professor Title Chief of Research, Division of General Internal Medicine Position Description The Division of General Internal Medicine at Washington University School of Medicine is seeking a visionary academic investigator (MD, PhD, or MD-PhD) to serve as inaugural Chief of Research — a role shaping the scientific identity of one of the School's fastest-growing and largest divisions. This is a unique opportunity to build a research enterprise from a position of strength: a newly consolidated Division spanning primary care, hospital medicine, geriatrics, and research with institutional commitment to growth. The Chief of Research will set strategic direction for the Division's research portfolio — expanding programs, recruiting talent, growing funded awards, and cultivating the next generation of physician-scientists through faculty development and trainee mentorship. This work is anchored within a School of Medicine ranked No. 2 nationally in NIH funding, offering unmatched access to institutional infrastructure and a deep culture of research excellence. We are particularly interested in funded investigators in health services research, clinical informatics, implementation science, and general medicine clinical research , with opportunities for cross-institutional collaboration. The role also carries broader opportunities including building research infrastructure across the Division's inpatient and outpatient programs, pioneering new capacity for community-engaged research, and implementing general medicine research training programs. This position offers scope to build interdisciplinary programs from the ground up, elevate the academic stature of general internal medicine within the School of Medicine, and directly shape the field's future. The Division backs this vision with resources for faculty recruitment and development. Basic Qualifications The successful candidate will be an accomplished investigator with a strong record of leadership, extramural funding, scholarship, and mentorship. Candidates should hold an M.D. and/or Ph.D. and have training in health services research, decision sciences, epidemiology, research ethics, informatics, or other areas relevant to our broad research mission. The ideal candidate will bring a demonstrated commitment to advancing the educational and scholarly missions of general internal medicine. Experience in academic leadership, scholarship, funded research, and education is preferred. Special Instructions The search committee will begin reviewing candidates immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Applications should include a detailed CV and a letter of interest that highlights the applicant’s personal vision and relevant leadership experience. To ensure full consideration, inquiries, nominations, and applications should be submitted electronically in confidence, to: Dr. Molly McNairy Chief, Division of General Internal Medicine mcnairy@wustl.edu Contact Name Dr. Molly McNairy Contact Email mcnairy@wustl.edu Posting Date 9/24/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011248": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Rheumatology on Clinician Track",
      "description": "Department Department of Medicine Division IM - Rheumatology Track Clinician Rank Assistant Professor Title Assistant Professor in Rheumatology on Clinician Track Position Description The Rheumatology Division in the Department of Medicine of the Washington University School of Medicine in St. Louis is seeking a BC/BE Rheumatologist to be appointed at the level of Assistant or Associate Professor. &nbsp; Washington University Rheumatology Division provides leading care for patients with rheumatic diseases (arthritis, musculoskeletal, autoimmune, and inflammatory conditions) and offers a level of experience and expertise available at few centers in the nation. We treat a wide range of common and complex rheumatic diseases including many types of arthritis and vasculitis, as well as other rare connective tissue diseases. Basic Qualifications Requirements and Essential Functions: Board Certified/Board Eligible Rheumatologist&nbsp; Successful completion of Internal Medicine Training and a Rheumatology Fellowship If desired to participate in teaching with protected time for clinical research and publications. Special Instructions Christine Pham, M.D., Division Chief Washington University School of Medicine Division of Rheumatology, 660 South Euclid, Campus Box 8045 St. Louis, MO&nbsp; 63110 cpham@wustl.edu Contact Name KELLY MARIE NESSEL Contact Email nesselk@wustl.edu Contact Phone 13143133631 Posting Date 8/28/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
