    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011249": {
      "title": "Chief, Section of Geriatrics",
      "description": "Department Department of Medicine Division General Internal Medicine Track Clinician Rank Associate Professor/Full Professor Title Chief, Section of Geriatrics Position Description Washington University School of Medicine, Division of General Internal Medicine seeks an accomplished academic geriatrician to lead and grow an innovative geriatrics program spanning clinical care, education (including a fellowship program), and research . The Section Chief will provide leadership for the Section of Geriatri cs , developing an overarching mission and guiding the section ’ s growth across clinical care, education, research, and community partnerships as one of the largest academic medical centers in the country . The role offers the opportunity to directly influence the future of geriatric care delivery, build interdisciplinary care models, strengthen the academic geriatrics program, and advance the section's standing within the School of Medicine and affiliated health system. The role also comes with funding to recruit faculty across the clinical and research enterprise and research support for candidates with a research portfolio . Basic Qualifications The successful candidate will be an accomplished academic geriatrician with a strong record of clinical leadership, scholarship, and mentorship. Candidates should hold an M.D. or D.O. degree and be board certified in Internal Medicine or Family Medicine with added qualification or board certification in Geriatric Medicine. The candidate should be eligible for unrestricted medical licensure in Missouri. The ideal candidate will bring a demonstrated commitment to advancing the clinical, educational, and scholarly missions of academic geriatrics. Experience in academic leadership, clinical operations, scholarship and research, and medical education is preferred. Contact Name Rachel Noon Contact Email rachel.noon@wustl.edu Contact Phone 3146435404 Posting Date 8/28/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
