    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011181": {
      "title": "CARDIOLOGY Clinical Chief of Cardiology, Division of Cardiology, Washington University School of Medicine",
      "description": "Department Department of Medicine Division IM - Cardiology Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title CARDIOLOGY Clinical Chief of Cardiology, Division of Cardiology, Washington University School of Medicine Position Description Reporting to the Division Chief of Cardiology, the Clinical Chief of Cardiology at Washington University School of Medicine (WashU Medicine) will be the physician leader for clinical cardiology practice growth and operations at WashU Medicine. &nbsp; The Clinical Chief will oversee clinical cardiology strategic planning, growth, and clinical operations for the Cardiology Division. The candidate will engage with Cardiology faculty, staff and trainees to recruit and retain outstanding clinical faculty and staff, optimize clinical operations, efficiency, clinical outcomes and patient experience. He/she will be responsible for developing and implementing plans for strategic growth and success of inpatient and outpatient clinical cardiology programs for Wash U Medicine, and will also oversee programs to optimize quality metrics, patient safety, and patient experience. &nbsp; Core responsibilities include clinical strategic planning, program development, operational oversight, integration of care delivery, and ensuring clinical excellence in alignment with the Division’s educational and research missions. The Clinical Chief will work closely with the Division Chief and Cardiology Section Heads, and Department of Medicine, School of Medicine’s Faculty Practice Plan (FPP), BJH and BJC HealthCare leaders. &nbsp; The candidate will oversee Cardiology Medical Directors based at BJH, BJWC, the Heart Care Institute and all Wash U Cardiology practice sites. The Clinical Chief will collaborate with other WashU Medicine Divisions and Departments, the FPP, BJC HealthCare, and community partners to optimize clinical growth and excellent multidisciplinary care. The Clinical Chief of Cardiology should be an experienced, outstanding practicing clinical cardiologist with operational and administrative experience establishing and growing clinical practices, overseeing clinical operations, improving quality and safety, leading physicians and working closely with hospitals to advance cardiovascular care. The candidate must have an active medical license, board certification in cardiology, and be eligible for medical licensure in Missouri and Illinois. &nbsp; Successful candidates will be expected to continue to practice clinically and be provided significant administrative time for the Clinical Chief role. To learn more about WashU Medicine, see :&nbsp; Washington University School of Medicine and the Division of Cardiology, see: Washington University Cardiovascular Division . Basic Qualifications Key Responsibilities: - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Clinical operations of the Division of Cardiology. Develop strategic growth plans and provide oversight for implementation of growth plans and inpatient and outpatient clinical operations. Optimize clinical operations, market share, clinical excellence and patient experience. Assure divisional compliance with all applicable laws and regulatory requirements including CMS, TJC, and the Missouri Department of Health. - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Serve as the primary liaison between the Cardiology division, WashU FPP leadership, and BJH/BJC HealthCare leadership to optimize quality and safety metrics in partnership with Cardiothoracic (CT) and Vascular surgery, Nursing, and Medical Directors. - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Set goals and implement initiatives to attain excellence in clinical outcomes, quality of care and patient experience to advance the Division’s regional and national prominence. - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In collaboration with the Chief of Cardiology, recruit and retain high-quality clinical faculty to address WashU/BJC system-wide cardiology growth plans and patient care needs. - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Oversee inpatient and outpatient cardiology services, throughput, and operations at all sites in collaboration with appropriate physician, nursing, and administrative leaders. - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Optimize clinic scheduling, physician and advanced practice provider productivity, template design, room utilization, referral standards, subspecialty triage, to maintain patient access and efficient patient care. - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Develop collaborative clinical programs encompassing cardiology subspecialties and partner disciplines (e.g., cardiac surgery, vascular surgery, radiology, maternal-fetal medicine, oncology, genetics, etc.). - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Collaborate with the Division Chief to develop short- and long-term clinical strategic plans. Assure adequate administrative and patient care infrastructure to support clinical endeavors. - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Maintain clear lines of communication; lead multidisciplinary administrative meetings to optimize patient care, patient experience and provider satisfaction. - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Oversee quality programs and quality director roles in the Division, including Quality Management meetings, and the distribution of meaningful use and quality metrics - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Establish an operating structure and processes that will advance and optimize efficiency and quality of care delivery so that it meets or surpasses national benchmarks (e.g., mortality, complications, remissions, outcomes, patient satisfaction and experience, etc.) - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Evaluate, document, and enhance the effectiveness of cardiovascular quality management programs across the WashU/BJH. Define and track metrics for access, experience, quality/safety (e.g., median new-patient wait days, on-time starts, LOS, readmissions, CG-CAHPS, etc). - &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Lead Cardiology performance and operational improvement programs to assure key milestones and indicators are within targets and participate in interdisciplinary improvement initiatives. Special Instructions Compensation: Highly competitive and commensurate with experience. Interested candidates should send a 1-2 page letter of interest and curriculum vitae to Sumanth Prabhu, MD, Lewin Distinguished Professor and Chief of Cardiology, Washington University School of Medicine, email: prabhu@wustl.edu, with copy to NikkiAnn Padilla, email: padilla@wustl.edu. Contact Name Mark Prosperi Contact Email prosperim@wustl.edu Contact Phone 314935-2844 Posting Date 1/21/2026 End Date No end date Reopened Date 2/5/2026 EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
