    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011124": {
      "title": "CARDIOLOGY: Sports Cardiology Program Director",
      "description": "Department Department of Medicine Division IM - Cardiology Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title CARDIOLOGY: Sports Cardiology Program Director Position Description The Division of Cardiology, Department of Medicine, at Washington University School of Medicine is conducting a search for an Assistant Professor, Associate Professor or Professor of Medicine to join the Sports Cardiology Program at Barnes-Jewish Hospital/Washington University School of Medicine and to lead the Program. The candidate should be an MD or MD/PhD with passionate about athlete heart health, and ready to lead a growing, dynamic program at one of the nation’s top academic medical centers. &nbsp; The candidate should be board-certified in adult cardiovascular disease. The next Director of Sports Cardiology will have the opportunity to build upon the strong foundation established since the program’s launch in 2020. As the first and only dedicated sports cardiology program in the St. Louis region, we’ve cultivated a consistent referral base spanning high school, collegiate, professional, and master athletes. Our newly developed cardiopulmonary exercise testing (CPET) program features state-of-the-art equipment and expanding clinical services. We’ve received enthusiastic support from both community and institutional partners, and this is an incredible opportunity to shape the next phase of a rapidly evolving field. The director will have the chance to lead clinical innovation, collaborate with multidisciplinary experts, and develop a high-impact program with regional and national visibility. Washington University and St Louis are recognized nationally and internationally for research in medicine, science, and biotechnology. St. Louis offers four seasons with an outstanding sports, cultural, and educational environment; it is a culturally diverse and family-oriented city. Basic Qualifications The candidate should be an MD or DO board-certified in adult cardiovascular disease&nbsp;and should be well-versed in ambulatory general cardiology. Interest in cardiac imaging, including 2-D transthoracic, transesophageal, 3D, and stress echocardiography is desirable. Inpatient cardiology time and diagnostic cardiac catheterization interest can be accommodated. Special Instructions Applicants should mail or e-mail to:&nbsp; Sumanth Prabhu, MD &nbsp;(prabhu@wustl.edu) his/her curriculum vitae and a letter stating career goals to the: Division of Cardiology, 660 South Euclid Avenue,&nbsp;MSC 8086-43-13, St. Louis, MO 63110. Contact Name Sumanth Prabhu Contact Email prabhu@wustl.edu Contact Phone 3143628908 Posting Date 6/9/2025 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1010636": {
      "title": "Bone Health Program Physician",
      "description": "Department Department of Medicine Division IM - Bone &amp; Mineral Diseases Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Bone Health Program Physician Position Description &nbsp; The Division of Bone and Mineral Metabolism at Washington University School of Medicine (WUSM) is seeking a BC/BE physician with expertise in bone and mineral disorders to join our world-class faculty in providing patient care, resident education and clinical research (if there is an interest). &nbsp; The practice will focus on care of patients with disorders of bone and mineral, metabolism, including osteoporosis, calcium, and phosphorus disorders in an outpatient setting. This is a flexible, part- or full-time position. &nbsp; We currently have a very busy clinical practice at three locations within the St. Louis metropolitan area. This position will be at a level commensurate with experience and requires a physician interested in multidisciplinary teamwork and dedication to teaching residents and fellows in a major academic clinical practice. Ranked among the top medical schools in the country by U.S. News &amp; World Report, Washington University School of Medicine and Barnes-Jewish Hospital offer a collegial environment that nurtures professional development and life-long learning. The Division of Bone and Mineral Diseases was established in 1975 with the foresight, leadership, and pioneering work of the late Louis V. Avioli, MD, one of the founding fathers of the field of bone and mineral diseases, laid the foundation of a unique, world-class center for research, training, and patient care dedicated to disorders of mineral and skeletal homeostasis. As the clinical component of the Division, the Washington University Bone Health Program offers a comprehensive approach to diagnosing, treating, and preventing osteoporosis and many other skeletal disorders, including rare or genetic bone diseases, and calcium or phosphorus disorders. The program was established in the early 1990s and has since represented one of the premier programs in the U.S. with this mission. The St Louis region is becoming a new center for entrepreneurship and a nucleus for innovation. &nbsp; The city has a patchwork of eclectic and affordable neighborhoods with outstanding nightlife, sport teams, quick commuting, public and private schools and award-winning national parks and museums. Competitive compensation and excellent benefit package are included. &nbsp; Basic Qualifications &nbsp; The position requires a Professional Medical Degree and certification or board eligibility by the American Board of Internal Medicine/Family Medicine/Obstetrics and Gynecology or other recognized/relevant post graduate training. &nbsp; Background experience in metabolic bone disorders or related specialties, including endocrinology, rheumatology, and women’s health is desirable. &nbsp; Special Instructions &nbsp; Interested applicants should send a letter of inquiry, their curriculum vitae, description of prior experience and future plans, and three letters of reference to: Linda Mudd Division of Bone and Mineral Diseases MSC 8301-04-11 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110 lmudd@wustl.edu &nbsp; Posting Date 1/1/2025 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1010630": {
      "title": "CARDIOLOGY: Faculty – Cardio-Oncology",
      "description": "Department Department of Medicine Division IM - Cardiology Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title CARDIOLOGY: Faculty – Cardio-Oncology Position Description &nbsp; The Division of Cardiology, Department of Medicine, at Washington University School of Medicine is conducting a search for an Instructor, Assistant Professor, Associate Professor or Professor of Medicine to join the Cardio-Oncology Center of Excellence at Barnes-Jewish Hospital/Washington University School of Medicine. The International Cardio-Oncology Society has recognized Washington University as a Gold Center of Excellence, the highest level of certification. The candidate should be an MD or MD/PhD who is board certified in Cardiovascular Disease. While some training in Cardio-Oncology is optimal, general cardiologists who have a strong interest in Cardio-Oncology are encouraged to apply. Washington University has a strong track record of training cardiologists who have transitioned to Cardio-Oncology during their career. Applicants would join a thriving and established practice with exposure to both inpatient and outpatient Cardio-Oncology and cardiac amyloidosis. Job specifics will be tailored to each applicant including relative time spent in outpatient versus inpatient care, medical education, and research time as well as ancillary services such as cardiac imaging. Washington University, Siteman Cancer Center, and St Louis are recognized nationally and internationally for research in medicine, science, and biotechnology. Rated Exceptional (the highest rating) by the National Cancer Institute as a Comprehensive Cancer Center, the Siteman Cancer Center is one of the five largest cancer centers in the country. The Cardio-Oncology Center of Excellence is strengthened by robust clinical and research collaboration between oncology, radiation oncology, and cardiology. St. Louis offers four seasons with an outstanding sport, cultural, and educational environment; it is a culturally diverse and family-oriented city. Applicants should direct e-mails to: Joshua Mitchell, MD (jdmitchell@wustl.edu), Director of Cardio-Oncology and Cardiac Amyloidosis or Sumanth Prabhu, MD (prabhu@wustl.edu), Chief of Cardiology, with his/ her curriculum vitae and a letter stating career goals. Please submit your application to: https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu. Select Department of Medicine; CARDIOLOGY: Faculty – Cardio- Oncology. &nbsp; Basic Qualifications The candidate should be an MD or DO board-certified in adult cardiovascular disease and should have an interest in Cardio-Oncology . Contact Name Dr. Josh Mitchell Contact Email jdmitchell@wustl.edu Contact Phone 3143628908 Posting Date 8/27/2024 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
