    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1010949": {
      "title": "Palliative Medicine Clinician",
      "description": "Department Department of Medicine Division IM - Palliative Medicine Track Clinician Rank Assistant Professor Title Palliative Medicine Clinician Position Description The Division of Palliative Medicine in the Department of Medicine at Washington University School of Medicine in St. Louis seeks applicants for a full-time Assistant or Associate Professor of Medicine. The successful applicant should have specific interest and/or experience in palliative medicine. The applicant will work in collaboration with a dynamic team of clinicians/investigators at Barnes-Jewish Hospital. Applicants should be boarded in palliative medicine and eligible for a medical license in Missouri. &nbsp; Additional qualifications in clinical research or medical education would be an asset. Interested applicants should send a letter of inquiry, their curriculum vitae, description of prior experience and future plans, and three letters of reference to: Lucas Jorgensen, Division Administrator, Jorgensenl@wustl.edu . Basic Qualifications Physician must hold M.D./D.O. form an accredited school, be board eligible or board certified in accordance with the American Board of Internal Medicine. Contact Name Lucas Jorgensen Contact Email jorgensenl@wustl.edu Posting Date 7/1/2024 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with S