    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011185": {
      "title": "Full-time Academic Sleep Medicine faculty",
      "description": "Department Department of Neurology Division Neurology Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Full-time Academic Sleep Medicine faculty Position Description Join the WashU Sleep Medicine Center! Full-Time Faculty Physician Opportunity The Department of Neurology at Washington University in St. Louis (WashU) is seeking an outstanding, full-time Academic Sleep Medicine faculty member (MD or MD/PhD) to join our Adult Sleep Medicine Section at the Instructor level, Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor rank. This is an exceptional opportunity to become part of a world-renowned academic institution at the forefront of clinical care, education, and research and work with other sleep clinicians and researchers at our multidisciplinary sleep center. About the Role: Clinical focus on outpatient sleep medicine evaluating patients with sleep disorders Interpretation of polysomnograms, multiple sleep latency tests, and home sleep apnea tests Teach and supervise fellows from our ACGME accredited sleep medicine fellowship and other trainees such as sleep technologists, medical students, and residents Participate in educational activities of our sleep center Engage in translational and clinical research, with collaborative opportunities across the Department of Neurology and our institution Our Center: The Washington University Multidisciplinary Sleep Medicine Center is a 12-bed, AASM-accredited sleep center Our team includes top specialists in neurology, pulmonary, internal medicine, otolaryngology, and psychology We offer comprehensive, multidisciplinary approaches to sleep medicine, including a growing sleep surgery program and a behavioral sleep medicine program Basic Qualifications Qualifications: DO, MD or MD/PhD Board Certified/Board Eligible (BC/BE) in Sleep Medicine (any primary specialty) Completed an ACGME accredited Sleep Medicine Fellowship Special Instructions Why WashU? Collaborate with leading experts in a supportive, inclusive academic environment Access to state-of-the-art facilities and robust research infrastructure Flexible schedules, generous vacation (22 days plus 8 holidays), and on-campus childcare Recognized for diversity, employee engagement, and satisfaction Join us in advancing the field of sleep medicine while making a profound impact on our patients and the St. Louis community. If you are passionate about clinical excellence, education, and research, we invite you to apply and become a valued member of our WashU Neurology Sleep Medicine team. Apply today to start your journey with WashU! Learn more about WashU Sleep Medicine: https://sleep.wustl.edu/ Contact Name Tracey Erdman Contact Email erdman@wustl.edu Contact Phone 314-747-0644 Posting Date 2/3/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
