    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1000008": {
      "title": "Clinical Faculty (Full-Time), Knight Alzheimer Disease Research Center (ADRC)",
      "description": "Department Department of Neurology Division Neurology Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Clinical Faculty (Full-Time), Knight Alzheimer Disease Research Center (ADRC) Position Description The Department of Neurology at Washington University School of Medicine in St. Louis and its Knight Alzheimer Disease Research Center (ADRC) are seeking a Board Certified/Board Eligible neurologist, psychiatrist, or geriatrician for a full-time faculty position as an Academic Clinician (clinical track). Responsibilities for this position include evaluating and managing patients with cognitive disorders in an outpatient subspecialty clinic, the Memory Diagnostic Center, and serving as the Knight ADRC site principal investigator for multi-center clinical trials, both those sponsored by academic entities and by industry, of experimental anti-Alzheimer therapies. Additional duties include teaching medical students, neurology residents, and Knight ADRC postdoctoral fellows; developing and leading funded clinical research programs are encouraged should interest and opportunity permit. Special Instructions Please submit electronically a letter of interest and Curriculum Vitae. Contact: John C. Morris, MD Director, Knight ADRC morrisj@abraxas.wustl.edu 314/286-2881 Contact Name John C. Morris, MD - Director, Knight ADRC Contact Email morrisj@abraxas.wustl.edu Contact Phone 314/286-2881 Posting Date 7/1/2025 End Date No end date Reopened Date 7/18/2025 EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
