    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011251": {
      "title": "POD 1 Physician Leadership Opportunity",
      "description": "Department Department of Anesthesiology Division Anesthesiology Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title POD 1 Physician Leadership Opportunity Position Description Overview This is an exciting opportunity to serve as a physician leader of a dynamic and complex clinical environment. POD 1 is based in Parkview Tower, on the main campus of Barnes-Jewish Hospital. The POD houses 13 operating rooms, including four state-of-the -art robotic ORs, and adjacent interventional radiology suites. The POD serves obstetrics and gynecology, colorectal, urologic and minimally invasive surgery. In this important leadership role, the Physician POD 1 leader will partner closely with BJH Perioperative Services and surgical colleagues to advance quality, safety, and efficiency in perioperative care. She or he will also plays a key role in fostering professionalism, teamwork, and innovation while supporting a safe and collaborative environment for patients and care teams. Key Responsibilities Include: Leading operational excellence efforts, including implementation of the WUDA staffing model Supporting high-quality, equitable, and timely care delivery in POD 1 Overseeing quality and safety initiatives and participating in PSQ efforts Collaborating with multidisciplinary teams across perioperative services Engaging in departmental leadership activities and ongoing leadership development Supporting a safe environment conducive to learning and innovations This is a clinical leadership opportunity with a stipend and departmental commitment to leadership development. The POD 1 leader will be a member of the WUDA BJH clinical leadership team and will report to the Director of Clinical Anesthesiology at BJH. Basic Qualifications Basic Qualifications: Full-time clinical faculty practicing primarily at BJH (including in-house call) ABA Board Certification in Anesthesiology Demonstrated clinical expertise and a strong commitment to safety, professionalism, and collaboration Special Instructions Interested physician faculty are encouraged to send their current CV and a brief statement of interest to Erin Kennedy at kennedye@wustl.edu . For additional information or questions, please contact Ivan Kangrga at kangrgai@wustl.edu Posting Date 9/4/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011246": {
      "title": "Medical Director, Pediatric Anesthesiology Children’s Specialty Care Center at CoxHealth – Springfield, Missouri",
      "description": "Department Department of Anesthesiology Division Pediatric Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Medical Director, Pediatric Anesthesiology Children’s Specialty Care Center at CoxHealth – Springfield, Missouri Position Description The Washington University Division of Pediatric Anesthesiology, in partnership with St. Louis Children’s Hospital and CoxHealth, is seeking a Board-Certified Pediatric Anesthesiologist to serve as the Medical Director of Anesthesiology for the newly established St. Louis Children’s Specialty Care Center at CoxHealth in Springfield, Missouri. This&nbsp;state-of-the-art outpatient clinic and surgery center will be the largest pediatric surgery center in Southwest Missouri, offering ambulatory surgery, imaging, laboratory services, infusion services, and pediatric specialty clinics. The Medical Director will provide clinical and administrative leadership to establish and grow pediatric anesthesiology services while ensuring exceptional patient care, operational excellence, and collaboration across departments. The Department of Anesthesiology seeks a collaborative physician leader who is committed to advancing its mission of delivering exceptional, patient-centered care; providing outstanding education and mentorship; advancing the science and clinical practice of anesthesiology; and fostering the professional development, engagement, and well-being of faculty and staff. The Pediatric Anesthesiology Medical Director will hold a primary faculty appointment in the Division of Pediatric Anesthesiology. The Medical Director will report to the Clinical Director and the Division Chief of Pediatric Anesthesiology; the Medical Director will oversee operations and anesthesia scheduling for the Pediatric operating room, the Ambulatory Procedure Center at St. Louis Children’s Specialty Care Center at CoxHealth, and Inpatient Pediatric Anesthesiology and sedation services at Cox Health. The individual will play a key role in developing clinical programs, establishing operational processes, promoting quality and patient safety initiatives, and strengthening partnerships among Washington University, St. Louis Children’s Hospital, and clinical leadership at CoxHealth to support the continued growth and success of the center. Key Responsibilities • Provide clinical and administrative leadership for pediatric anesthesiology services at the Children’s Specialty Care Center. • Deliver high-quality, evidence-based pediatric anesthesia care while maintaining the highest standards of patient safety and clinical excellence. • Collaborate with surgeons, nursing leaders, administrators, and multidisciplinary teams to support coordinated patient care and operational success. • Oversee clinical operations, including scheduling and operating room block-time planning. • Develop, implement, and monitor local clinical protocols and quality improvement initiatives. • Partner with executive leadership to support strategic planning, operational efficiency, and program growth. • Foster a culture of innovation, teamwork, professional development, and continuous improvement. • Support the educational and clinical missions of Washington University, St. Louis Children’s Hospital, and CoxHealth. • Promote physician engagement, staff well-being, and a positive work environment. • Ensure compliance with applicable regulatory, accreditation, and institutional standards. Quality and Safety • Provide leadership for quality, safety, and service initiatives to ensure the delivery of safe, high-quality, patient-centered pediatric anesthesiology care. • Support alignment of Washington University Department of Anesthesiology (WUDA) with BJC HealthCare and Washington University School of Medicine (WUSM) perioperative patient safety and quality initiatives. • Ensure WUDA achieves Safety, Quality, and Service metrics as outlined in the Contracted Services Agreement. • Partner with clinical and operational leaders to ensure anesthesiology services support WUDA Patient Safety, Quality (PSQ), and Green initiatives. • Develop, implement, and maintain a comprehensive Quality Assurance/Quality Improvement (QA/QI) program for the Children's Specialty Care Center in collaboration with the Division Chief. • Monitor clinical quality, patient safety, and operational performance through data-driven quality improvement initiatives, performance metrics, and ongoing program evaluation. • Standardize clinical policies, protocols, and procedures across relevant perioperative services to promote consistent, evidence-based, high-quality care. • Foster a culture of continuous improvement, patient safety, transparency, and accountability by engaging physicians, advanced practice providers, nursing, and multidisciplinary teams in quality and safety initiatives. About Washington University School of Medicine WashU Medicine is a global leader in biomedical research, patient care, and medical education, with more than 3,000 faculty members. Its National Institutes of Health (NIH) research funding portfolio is among the largest of U.S. medical schools, supporting groundbreaking discoveries and innovation across biomedical science. WashU Medicine continues to advance healthcare through significant investments in research, education, clinical training, and physician development. Its faculty physicians provide care at leading academic and community healthcare facilities throughout the region. About St. Louis Children’s Hospital St. Louis Children’s Hospital, part of BJC HealthCare, has provided exceptional pediatric care for more than 140 years and serves children from all 50 states and more than 80 countries worldwide. In partnership with WashU Medicine, St. Louis Children’s Hospital is consistently ranked among the nation’s best pediatric hospitals and is recognized for excellence in comprehensive pediatric services, including heart care, cancer care, neurology, orthopedics, and trauma services. The hospital is among the few pediatric hospitals in the Midwest recognized by the American College of Surgeons as a Level 1 Pediatric Trauma Center. About Springfield, Missouri Springfield offers an outstanding quality of life with a welcoming community, affordable living, excellent schools, and abundant opportunities for recreation and entertainment. Located in the heart of the Ozarks, the region provides access to outdoor activities, cultural attractions, and a vibrant community environment. Basic Qualifications Required Qualifications: • M.D. or D.O. degree. • Board Certification in Anesthesiology. • Board Certification in Pediatric Anesthesiology. • Missouri medical license (or eligibility). • Missouri BNDD license. • DEA registration. • PALS certification. • Specialty certification maintained through the MOCA program. • U.S. citizenship or authorization to work in the United States. Additional Qualifications The ideal candidate is a highly skilled pediatric anesthesiologist with demonstrated leadership abilities, strong communication and collaboration skills, and a commitment to clinical excellence. The successful candidate will be a collaborative leader and innovative thinker, with proven results and benchmarks, who is dedicated to advancing pediatric anesthesiology care while supporting colleagues, trainees, and staff. About the Division of Pediatric Anesthesiology at WashU Medicine The Division of Pediatric Anesthesiology at WashU Medicine is nationally recognized for excellence in clinical care, education, research, and innovation. As part of the Department of Anesthesiology, the division provides comprehensive anesthetic care for infants, children, and adolescents across a wide range of surgical and procedural specialties. Faculty members are dedicated to advancing the field through innovative research, educating the next generation of anesthesiologists, and delivering exceptional patient-centered care. The division fosters a collaborative and supportive environment that values professional development, clinical excellence, innovation, and the well-being of its faculty and staff. Special Instructions Application Process Interested candidates should submit: • One-page cover letter • Curriculum Vitae (CV) • Names and contact information for three professional references Applications may be submitted through the Washington University Faculty Opportunities portal https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/apply Contact Information Dolores Njoku, MD The Wise Professor of Anesthesiology Division Chief, Pediatric Anesthesiology and Vice Chair Department of Anesthesiology Washington University School of Medicine Pediatric Anesthesiologist-in-Chief St. Louis Children’s Hospital Email: njoku@wustl.edu Contact Name Dolores Njoku Contact Email njoku@wustl.edu Posting Date 8/27/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011242": {
      "title": "Integrated Program for Anesthesiology Specialists",
      "description": "Department Department of Anesthesiology Division Anesthesiology Track Clinician Rank Assistant Professor Title Integrated Program for Anesthesiology Specialists Position Description The Integrated Program for Anesthesiology Specialists (IPAS) in the Department of Anesthesiology offers academically accomplished anesthesiologists an opportunity to share advanced clinical skills and unique experiences at one of the&nbsp;most successful and innovative anesthesiology programs in the U.S.&nbsp;This initiative fosters teaching opportunities, clinical growth, and academic advancement by bringing in outstanding talent to help advance the missions of the department.&nbsp; IPAS participants serve as departmental faculty, practicing clinically at Barnes-Jewish Hospital. Key responsibilities include teaching and supervising trainees across a diverse patient care environment. Faculty balance clinical care with protected time for academic pursuits, including education, quality improvement, and research. They also contribute to the department’s educational programs through lectures, clinical conferences, journal clubs, and clinical simulation cases. WashU Medicine’s Department of Anesthesiology has a rich history of excellence across patient care, education, and research. Serving a large tertiary care clinical practice, WashU Medicine treats complex patients across all specialties. The department actively advances anesthesiology and perioperative outcomes through meaningful discovery, consistently ranking among the top three anesthesiology departments in funding from the National Institutes of Health (NIH). Basic Qualifications Qualifications for this program include: At least 4+ years of training in clinical anesthesiology Board certification in anesthesiology in the applicant's home country Evidence of involvement in research, academic projects, quality improvement, or educational initiatives Advanced or unique clinical experience that enhances the missions of the department USMLEs desired but not required Special Instructions Interested candidates should complete the application process by clicking Apply below and submit the following:&nbsp; a one-page cover letter detailing 1) alignment of this program toward academic career advancement, 2) goals/plans for academic time, 3) potential metors/collaborators if known. curriculum vitae, and names and contact information of 3 references Contact Email joinanest@wustl.edu Posting Date 8/11/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011237": {
      "title": "Pediatric Pain &amp; Regional Anesthesiologist",
      "description": "Department Department of Anesthesiology Division Pediatric Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Pediatric Pain &amp; Regional Anesthesiologist Position Description The Department of Anesthesiology at Washington University School of Medicine is seeking a full-time Pediatric Pain &amp; Regional Physician to join the Division of Pediatric Anesthesiology and Pain Medicine. This is an outstanding opportunity to become part of a nationally recognized academic medical center dedicated to providing exceptional, family-centered care to infants, children, and adolescents across the full spectrum of perioperative and pain management. The successful candidate will have a diverse clinical practice that includes inpatient acute pain management, outpatient chronic pain medicine, and pediatric operating room anesthesia. Clinical responsibilities include the evaluation and management of children with postoperative and medically complex pain, chronic and complex pediatric pain conditions, and the provision of anesthetic care for a broad range of pediatric surgical procedures. The position offers opportunities to perform regional anesthesia and interventional pain procedures consistent with the candidate's training and expertise. You will work within a collaborative, multidisciplinary team that includes pediatric anesthesiologists, pain physicians, surgeons, pediatric subspecialists, psychologists, rehabilitation specialists, advanced practice providers, nurses, and other healthcare professionals to deliver comprehensive, evidence-based care for children with acute, chronic, cancer-related, and complex pain conditions. The Division provides clinical care at the nationally ranked St. Louis Children's Hospital, Shriners Children's St. Louis, and Children's Specialty Care Centers in West and South Counties. Together, our pediatric anesthesiology team includes 34 pediatric anesthesiologists, 28 CRNAs, pediatric anesthesiology fellows, 4 nurse practitioners, and 5 administrators, performing approximately 30,000 anesthetics annually across our clinical sites. As part of Washington University School of Medicine, the Division shares the Department of Anesthesiology's academic mission by providing outstanding clinical care while educating and mentoring medical students, anesthesiology residents, and pediatric anesthesiology fellows. Faculty are encouraged to participate in clinical and translational research, quality improvement initiatives, and program development. The Department is deeply committed to supporting faculty growth, leadership development, and career advancement at every stage. The ideal candidate is board-certified in Pediatric Anesthesiology, has successfully completed a Pediatric Anesthesiology fellowship, and also has formal training in Pediatric Pain Medicine or Pediatric Regional Anesthesia, through fellowship or equivalent advanced clinical training and experience. &nbsp; &nbsp; About Us The Washington University in Saint Louis Department of Anesthesiology and Pain Medicine has over 200 faculty members and is a national and international leader in clinical care, innovation and research.&nbsp; The medical center is geographically located in a beautiful setting next to Forest Park, a 1300-acre facility that is one of the largest urban parks in the United States! St. Louis is a vibrant metropolitan area with multiple eclectic neighborhoods, a healthy arts environment, a foodie scene as well as nationally ranked professional baseball, soccer, and hockey teams. For more information, kindly visit the Pediatric Anesthesiology Division Website: https://anesthesiology.wustl.edu/patient-care/pediatric-anesthesiology/ , the St. Louis Children’s Hospital Website: &nbsp; https://www.stlouischildrens.org/ and the following websites for St. Louis fun activities:&nbsp; https://www.explorestlouis.com and https://thestl.com Basic Qualifications Board Certification in Anesthesiology Board Certification in Pediatric Anesthesiology Applicants with a strong commitment to building a collaborative culture and the exploration of novel approaches to fellowship training are encouraged &nbsp; Applicants with a management style that emphasizes teamwork, communication, collegiality, flexibility, trust and patience are encouraged Special Instructions Interested applicants should send a letter of interest and their curriculum vitae to Dr. Dolores Njoku.&nbsp; &nbsp; Contact Information: Dolores Njoku, MD The Wise Professor of Anesthesiology Division Chief, Pediatric Anesthesiology and Vice Chair Department of Anesthesiology Washington University School of Medicine Pediatric Anesthesiologist-in-Chief St. Louis Children’s Hospital njoku@wustl.edu Contact Name Dolores Njoku Contact Email njoku@wustl.edu Posting Date 7/29/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
