    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011165": {
      "title": "Director of Anesthesiology at Sunset Hills Ambulatory Surgery Center (ASC)",
      "description": "Department Department of Anesthesiology Division Anesthesiology Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Director of Anesthesiology at Sunset Hills Ambulatory Surgery Center (ASC) Position Description WashU Medicine in St. Louis, Department of Anesthesiology (WUDA) is seeking applications for the Medical Director Position at the Sunset Hills Ambulatory Surgery Center (ASC). This clinical leadership role will partner with and assist the Regional and Ambulatory Division Chief and the Vice Chair for Clinical Operations to support daily operations, scheduling faculty and CRNAs accordingly with OR case mix/volume, and promote safety, quality, and efficiency of healthcare delivery in Sunset Hills ASC operating rooms and procedural areas. The Medical Director of the Sunset Hills ASC will have an important role in supporting WUDA clinical teams; in furthering professionalism and teamwork, diversity, inclusion and belonging, learning and innovation, and ensuring that BJH perioperative and procedural areas are a respectful, safe, and nurturing environment for patients and members of the healthcare team. Key Responsibilities: Operational Excellence Partner with Sunset Hills ASC Perioperative Services and other leaders in managing daily schedules and hours of operation. Provide around-the-clock logistical support to WUDA teams on the Sunset Hills ASC campus. Ensure WUDA provides highly reliable, high-quality, equitable, and timely care in perioperative and procedural areas. Advance all four of the department’s quadripartite missions through alignment of clinical operations with education, research, and service goals. Partner with the Chair, Vice Chairs, Division Chief, and clinical staff in developing strategic plans and formulating policies that strengthen departmental operations. In partnership with the departmental financial team, assure sound financial management and stewardship of resources. Quality and Safety Support WUDA alignment with BJC and WUSM perioperative Patient Safety and Quality initiatives. Ensure WUDA meets Safety, Quality, and Service metrics as outlined in the Contracted Services agreement. Ensure clinical operations support of WUDA PSQ and Green initiatives. Create and maintain a QA/QI process for Sunset Hills ASC, in partnership with the Division Chief. Standardize policies and procedures across relevant clinical areas to ensure consistent, high-quality care delivery. Leadership Foster a collaborative, supportive culture; build clinical teams and a sense of community among WUSM and BJC clinical staff. Represent WUDA with BJC Perioperative Services, WUSM surgical, and procedural services. In partnership with the Vice Chair for Clinical Operations and Division Chief, advise on staffing needs including optimal staffing models and recruitment priorities and initiatives. Contribute actively to recruitment and retention efforts of both anesthesiologists and CRNAs. Basic Qualifications Full time clinical faculty ABA Board Certification in Anesthesiology Recognized for impeccable integrity, a role model for colleagues and trainees Exceptional emotional intelligence and communication skills Strong commitment to clinical excellence and culture of safety Commitment to building a collaborative, respectful and inclusive, patient-centric culture Leadership style characterized by the ability to create a common vision for diverse constituents and to build consensus Ownership and accountability for ideas, actions, and outcomes Proficiency in ultrasound-guided regional anesthesia techniques including indwelling peripheral nerve catheters Special Instructions Interested physician faculty are encouraged to send their current CV and a brief statement of interest to Kayla Hewitt (hewittk@wustl.edu), Administrative Assistant to Mitchell Fingerman, MD, FASA. For additional information or questions, please contact Paul Knocke at knocke@wustl.eduand visit https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/.&nbsp; Posting Date 11/25/2025 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011151": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Professor in Pain Research / Clinical Pain Psychology",
      "description": "Department Department of Anesthesiology Division WU Pain Center Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Assistant, Associate, or Professor in Pain Research / Clinical Pain Psychology Position Description We have an excellent opportunity for a pain psychologist to join our interventional, multidisciplinary Pain Management Division, within the Department of Anesthesiology, Washington University, St. Louis. The mission of the Washington University Pain Center ( https://pain.wustl.edu/ ) is to alleviate human suffering from pain through integration of research, education, and clinical practice. Our clinical work is spread across the St. Louis area including sites at Barnes-Jewish Hospital, Missouri Baptist Medical Center, and Barnes-Jewish West County Hospital. The available position is for a Missouri state licensed pain psychologist, with a working knowledge of psychiatric diagnosis and treatment, chronic pain conditions, and evidence-based interventions with communication and interpersonal skills. This open rank position is available on the clinical track. New faculty members receive an initial two-year clinical guarantee of salary and bonus, to ensure adequate income while establishing a practice, but there is also opportunity for productivity-based compensation. Candidates will be expected to hold a part-time pain psychology clinical position at our multidisciplinary pain management center and will also be expected to direct an active extramurally funded research program. &nbsp; Applications from candidates conducting clinical and/or translational research are encouraged. &nbsp; Basic Qualifications Clinical Expectations • Provide individual, group psychotherapy and crisis intervention for patients.&nbsp; • Perform diagnostic interviews and assessments and prepare documentation of results.&nbsp; • Prepare progress notes and billing documentation in accordance with department and professional guidelines.&nbsp; • Provide consultation services to medical staff and faculty.&nbsp; • Participate in interdisciplinary treatment team meetings.&nbsp; • Assist in the education and supervision of medical students, residents, and fellows.&nbsp; Research Qualifications • PhD or MD with postdoctoral experience Contact Name Melissa Bowman and Anne DeSchryver Contact Email melissabowman@wustl.edu Posting Date 9/22/2025 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
