    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1010946": {
      "title": "Diagnostic Radiologist - Remote",
      "description": "Department Department of Radiology Division Diagnostic Radiology Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Diagnostic Radiologist - Remote Position Description We are currently seeking a Diagnostic Remote Radiologist for an exciting and rewarding opportunity at the prestigious Mallinckrodt Institute of Radiology at Washington University. The chosen candidate will be providing remote coverage for Phelps County Regional Medical Center. - Monday to Friday, 7AM to 4PM.&nbsp; - No nights or call coverage - Ability to earn additional compensation for paid weekend and off hours coverage. Basic Qualifications - Provide expert radiological diagnosis primarily for inpatient and ER general coverage. - Analyze and interpret outpatient radiographs, ultrasound and CT scans. - Collaborate with other medical staff to improve patient care. - Provide timely and high-quality reports and consultation on patient findings. - Participate in multidisciplinary conferences to recommend patient treatment plans. - Adhere to all departmental procedures and quality standards. Additional Qualifications - Board-certified or board-eligible in Diagnostic Radiology. - Ability to work remotely and maintain a high level of productivity and quality. - Proficiency in interpreting ultrasound and CT scans. - No MR interpretation required. Please note: This position is not visa eligible. Special Instructions Benefits: - 22 vacation days with credit for holidays worked. - Comprehensive healthcare coverage. - Generous 403b matching. - Incidence-based malpractice insurance. - College tuition benefit for dependents. We are committed to offering competitive compensation and a comprehensive benefits package. If you are looking to work at a top-tier institute and have the necessary skills and experience, we would love to hear from you. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, CV, and contact information for three references. Apply today and join our team of professionals dedicated to excellence in patient care Washington University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, disability or veteran status. Contact Name Dr. Pamela Woodard Contact Email woodardp@wustl.edu Posting Date 5/1/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
