    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011214": {
      "title": "Instructor of Surgery (Division of Surgical Sciences)",
      "description": "Department Department of Surgery Division Surgical Sciences Track Research Rank Instructor Title Instructor of Surgery (Division of Surgical Sciences) Position Description The role will be primarily expected to develop and conduct new microsurgical procedures to facilitate the identification of mechanisms underlying lung transplant rejection. You will also be responsible for teaching microsurgical techniques to new staff, graduate students, and postgraduate fellows interested in pursuing research-focused careers. Other duties will include preparing manuscripts based on data generated by these models and helping develop new funding opportunities by assisting with grant proposal development. Finally, you will continue your intravital microscope studies to identify and study inflammatory cell behavior that causes lung transplant injury and rejection. Basic Qualifications MD, PhD Posting Date 4/21/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011197": {
      "title": "Heart &amp; Lung Procurement Surgeon",
      "description": "Department Department of Surgery Division Cardiothoracic Surgery Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Heart &amp; Lung Procurement Surgeon Position Description Washington University School of Medicine in Saint Louis &amp; Barnes-Jewish Hospital is seeking a Heart and Lung Procurement Surgeon to support its expanding thoracic transplant and donor recovery program. The selected candidate will perform surgical recovery of donor thoracic organs, including heart, lungs, and heart–lung blocks, and collaborate with transplant teams and organ procurement organizations to ensure optimal organ preservation and successful transplantation outcomes. This position will commensurate as Instructor/ Assistant Professor level in Department of Surgery, depending on the entry level and offers an opportunity to work within a highly collaborative, multidisciplinary transplant environment while contributing directly to life-saving organ transplantation. Responsibilities Perform surgical procurement of donor hearts and lungs for transplantation. Conduct donor organ assessment, including intraoperative evaluation of thoracic organs. Manage organ preservation, packaging, and transport according to transplant protocols. Participate in procurement travel to regional donor hospitals as required. Work closely with Organ Procurement Organizations (OPOs), transplant coordinators, and cardiothoracic surgical teams. Assist with documentation and regulatory compliance for transplant programs. Participate in on-call coverage for thoracic organ recovery. Contribute to quality improvement initiatives and transplant program development. Basic Qualifications Required MD or DO (or international equivalent). Completion of accredited cardiothoracic surgery training. Board-certified or board-eligible in Cardiothoracic Surgery. Eligible for state medical licensure in Missouri. Ability to travel for donor procurement procedures. Preferred Prior experience in thoracic organ procurement or cardiothoracic transplantation. Familiarity with donation after circulatory death (DCD) protocols . Experience with ex-vivo organ perfusion systems or advanced preservation techniques. Special Instructions About the Program Washington University School of Medicine in Saint Louis &amp; Barnes-Jewish Hospital is a leading transplant center dedicated to advancing cardiothoracic surgery and organ transplantation. Our multidisciplinary transplant team performs annually about 70 heart transplantation, 80 lung transplantation, and is committed to clinical excellence, innovation, and research. We do donation after circulatory death (DCD) heart &nbsp; procurements, normothermic regional perfusion, as well as cold continuous machine perfusion. Interested applicants should submit the following: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Contact information for three professional references. Contact: Dr. Amit Pawale at amitp@wustl.edu Contact Name Susie Heck Contact Email susie.heck@wustl.edu Posting Date 3/12/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
