    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011105": {
      "title": "Professor/Associate Professor - Division of Cardiothoracic Surgery",
      "description": "Department Department of Surgery Division Cardiothoracic Surgery Track Clinician Rank Associate Professor/Full Professor Title Professor/Associate Professor - Division of Cardiothoracic Surgery Position Description The Division of Cardiothoracic Surgery at Washington University School of Medicine in St. Louis is seeking a General Thoracic Surgeon to join our nationally recognized program. New graduates are welcome to apply. This position is based at Memorial Hospital Belleville, 4500 Memorial Drive, Belleville, IL 62226 This position Job Details • The primary focus of this position will be clinical activities spanning the breadth of general thoracic surgery. • With our program covering multiple facilities, this position will provide clinical care primarily at one affiliate location with secondary responsibilities at other locations (including Barnes Jewish Hospital), if needed. The affiliate location has an established Cardiothoracic surgery program. • An important part of the position will be a leadership role representing the Division of Cardiothoracic Surgery in the lung cancer screening programs across the entire Barnes Jewish Corporation healthcare system. • Academic rank and salary commensurate with experience. • Opportunity to participate in the lung transplant program if interested &nbsp; Benefits • Competitive salary and benefits package • Health, dental, and vision insurance • Retirement plan options • Generous paid time off (PTO) and holidays • Professional development and continuing education support • Collaborative work environment • Opportunities for research, teaching, and leadership roles The St. Louis region St. Louis is a vibrant and growing metropolitan area offering an exceptional quality of life. Known for its rich history, diverse culture, and friendly community, the region boasts a low cost of living, excellent schools, and a wealth of recreational activities. From the iconic Gateway Arch to world-class museums, theaters, and sports teams, St. Louis offers something for everyone. Whether you enjoy outdoor adventures, fine dining, or family-friendly events, you'll find a welcoming environment with ample opportunities to thrive professionally and personally. Join us in this dynamic city, where work-life balance and a strong sense of community come together. Basic Qualifications • Completion of an ACGME Thoracic Surgery Fellowship Program • Candidates must be board-certified or board-eligible • Ability to obtain Missouri license • Minimally invasive or robotic surgery skills in general thoracic surgery Special Instructions Interested candidates are invited to submit a resume and cover letter to CV to Dr. Varun Puri, Chief of Thoracic Surgery, via our department contact Ms. Susie Heck, s usie.heck@wustl.edu Posting Date 1/8/2025 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1010786": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Otolaryngology - Facial Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery",
      "description": "Department Department of Otolaryngology Division Otolaryngology Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Assistant/Associate Professor of Otolaryngology - Facial Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery Position Description The Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery at Washington University School of Medicine in St. Louis, MO is seeking a Board certified or Board eligible physician who has completed a facial plastic and reconstructive surgery fellowship.&nbsp;&nbsp;This position allows for opportunities to practice in St. Louis City and St. Louis County at an academic tertiary care medical center, a separate facial plastic and reconstructive surgery office and a surgery center. The Department of Otolaryngology currently comprises &gt;50 clinical and research faculty and a top-tier otolaryngology residency training program. Teaching of fellows, residents and medical students is expected. The department has vast opportunities to provide cutting-edge patient care in addition to basic, translational and clinical research experience. Candidates with a strong track record of funded research may be considered for protected research time. Collaboration with existing departmental clinical and basic science investigators is encouraged.&nbsp; Salary commensurate with experience.&nbsp; Basic Qualifications Candidates must be board certified or eligible for certification and must be able to obtain a Missouri State license. Contact Name John Chi, MD Contact Email johnchi@wustl.edu Posting Date 8/18/2026 End Date No end date Reopened Date 8/18/2026 EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
