    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011239": {
      "title": "Clinical Faculty - Full Time: Orthopedic Sports Medicine Surgeon",
      "description": "Department Department of Orthopedic Surgery Division Orthopaedic Surgery Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Clinical Faculty - Full Time: Orthopedic Sports Medicine Surgeon Position Description Position summary The Department of Orthopaedic Surgery at Washington University School of Medicine is seeking a full-time orthopedic sports medicine surgeon to join its nationally recognized Sports Medicine Division. The successful candidate will develop a high-volume academic sports medicine practice with a particular interest in hip arthroscopy. The position offers opportunities for clinical growth, resident and fellow education, research and participation in the continued development of the division’s regenerative medicine program. Clinical responsibilities The orthopedic sports medicine surgeon will: · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Develop and maintain a full-time academic sports medicine practice · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Build a clinical focus in hip arthroscopy · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Provide operative and nonoperative care for athletes and other active patients · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Work closely with specialists across the department’s 12 clinical divisions to provide comprehensive musculoskeletal care · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Participate in the continued growth of the regenerative medicine program · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Contribute to the clinical and academic activities of the Sports Medicine Division · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Support the continued growth and development of sports medicine services WashU Medicine Sports Medicine has a regional, national and international reputation for excellence in the operative and nonoperative care of active patients, including sports-related concussion care. Education and teaching responsibilities The faculty member will actively participate in orthopedic resident and fellow education, including: · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Clinical instruction and supervision · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Teaching in the operating room and outpatient setting · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Participation in conferences, journal clubs and other educational activities · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Mentorship of residents, fellows and medical students Research responsibilities The faculty member will conduct research based on their interests and academic goals. Areas may include: · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Clinical research · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Basic science · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Translational research · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Biomechanics · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Outcomes research The department supports collaboration with researchers across Washington University School of Medicine. Experienced research coordinators and departmental resources are available to assist with research development and implementation. &nbsp; Basic Qualifications Applicants must: Have completed or be in the process of completing an accredited sports medicine fellowship Be board certified or board eligible in orthopedic surgery Be eligible to obtain a permanent Missouri medical license Demonstrate a commitment to excellent patient care Demonstrate an interest in resident, fellow and medical student education Have an interest in clinical, basic science, translational, biomechanics or outcomes research Work effectively within a multidisciplinary academic practice Additional Qualifications About the practice Washington University Orthopedics is a nationally recognized academic orthopedic practice serving patients throughout the St.&nbsp;Louis region and beyond. Practice highlights include: · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; More than 230,000 patient visits annually · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; More than 20,000 surgeries annually · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; More than 100 orthopedic specialists · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 13 clinical locations across the region · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Barnes-Jewish Hospital and St.&nbsp;Louis Children’s Hospital, both Level I trauma centers · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Team physician relationships with the St.&nbsp;Louis Blues, St.&nbsp;Louis CITY SC and other area teams St.&nbsp;Louis is home to a strong academic and medical community and serves as a regional center for research, education and advanced patient care. Compensation and benefits Washington University provides comprehensive and competitive compensation and benefits, including: · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Competitive base compensation with productivity-based earning potential · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Year-end bonus opportunity based on division profitability · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Retirement savings plan · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Health, dental and vision insurance · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Health savings and flexible spending accounts · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Long-term disability and life insurance · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tuition benefits for employees, spouses and dependent children · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Discretionary allowance for approved professional and educational expenses · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Work-life and family-care resources · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Free MetroLink and bus pass For additional information about Washington University benefits, visit hr.wustl.edu . Our mission and vision Vision: To redefine excellence in Orthopaedic Surgery and Rehabilitation. Mission: To have a national and international impact on orthopedics through excellence in clinical care, education, research and leadership. Department distinctions include: · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; No.&nbsp;1 in Missouri for adult orthopedics, U.S. News &amp; World Report · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; No.&nbsp;4 in the United States for orthopedic residency training, Doximity · &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; No.&nbsp;3 in the United States for NIH-funded orthopedic research, BRIMR Special Instructions How to apply Interested candidates should submit a letter of interest and curriculum vitae to: Kelly Thies Administrative Professional for Robert Brophy, MD Division Chief, Sports Medicine Washington University School of Medicine 314-514-3569 kthies@wustl.edu Contact Name Kelly Thies Contact Email kthies@wustl.edu Contact Phone 314-514-3569 Posting Date 7/30/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
