    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011201": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Instructor Pediatrics Rheumatology #1011201",
      "description": "Department Department of Pediatrics Division Peds Rheumatology Track Tenure Rank Instructor Title Tenure-Track Instructor Pediatrics Rheumatology #1011201 Position Description This position is for a physician-scientist with a track record of successful research that has strong potential to lead to the development of an independent research program. Basic Qualifications Board certified or board eligible in Pediatric Rheumatology. Demonstrated success in rheumatology-related research including grants, presentations, and/or publications that will lead to an independent research program Additional Qualifications Career development or independent research grant(s). &nbsp; Training in IM/Peds. Contact Name Debra Warren Contact Email debrawarren@wustl.edu Contact Phone 314-826-2795 Posting Date 4/6/2026 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
