    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011116": {
      "title": "Clinical Geneticist Faculty - Pediatric Genetics #1011116",
      "description": "Department Department of Pediatrics Division Peds Genetics &amp; Genomic Medicine Track Clinician Rank Assistant Professor Title Clinical Geneticist Faculty - Pediatric Genetics #1011116 Position Description The Division of Medical Genetics and Genomics is seeking a clinical geneticist with interest in RASopathies to join our practice at the rank of Assistant Professor. The candidate will create a clinical RASopathies program and serve as associate program director for the genetics residency training programs.&nbsp; Basic Qualifications MD or equivalent degree, American Board of Medical Genetics and Genomics board certified or board eligible. Additional Qualifications PhD and expertise and clinical and research interest in RASopathies including Noonan syndrome. Contact Name Patricia Dickson Contact Email pdickson@wustl.edu Posting Date 3/20/2025 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
