    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1011077": {
      "title": "Pediatric Cardiology Echo/Fetal Echo Attending #1011077",
      "description": "Department Department of Pediatrics Division Pediatrics-Cardiology/Heart Stat Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Pediatric Cardiology Echo/Fetal Echo Attending #1011077 Position Description The Department of Pediatrics&nbsp; at Washington University School of Medicine in St. Louis, is seeking a pediatric cardiologist to join our team of 26 clinicians and basic scientists, based at St. Louis Children’s Hospital. &nbsp; We seek to recruit a Pediatric Echocardiologist/Fetal Echocardiologist to join our echocardiology attending faculty team. The &nbsp;ideal candidate will be board eligible/certified in Pediatric Cardiology.&nbsp; The candidate will have advanced training or expertise in non-invasive imaging and/or fetal echocardiography. Primary responsibilities will include echocardiography and/or fetal echocardiology consultations, development and expansion of fetal cardiology outreach sites, and collaborating with multidisciplinary specialists for fetal evaluations and interventions in the rapidly growing Fetal Care Center, a partnership of Washington University School of Medicine, Barnes-Jewish Hospital, and St. Louis Children’s Hospital.&nbsp; We have a widening fetal cardiology referral base with a three-fold increase in fetal echocardiogram volume over the past 5 years. Additional responsibilities will include interpretation of other non-invasive imaging (transthoracic and transesophageal echocardiography), ambulatory and inpatient cardiology consultations.&nbsp; &nbsp; Washington University School of Medicine is consistently ranked as one of the best medical schools in the country and is a longstanding leader in funding for pediatric research. St. Louis Children’s Hospital is a 400 bed free-standing children’s hospital established in 1879. The St. Louis Children’s and Washington University Heart Center includes an active surgical program, a 16-bed Cardiac Intensive Care Unit, and one of the nation’s largest pediatric heart failure and heart transplantation programs. &nbsp; &nbsp; Basic Qualifications The ideal candidate must be eligible for licensure in Missouri, be board certified (or eligible) in pediatric cardiology and have had advanced training in pediatric cardiac electrophysiology. Candidates will be considered at the Assistant or Associate Professor level. Special Instructions Please contact Dr. Andrew Glatz with any questions related to this posting. Contact Name Andrew Glatz Contact Email glatz@wustl.edu Contact Phone (314) 454-4561 Posting Date 9/24/2024 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
