    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1010977": {
      "title": "# 1010977 - Cardiac Intensivist",
      "description": "Department Department of Pediatrics Division Pediatrics-Cardiology/Heart Stat Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title # 1010977 - Cardiac Intensivist Position Description The positions include an appointment at appropriate rank in the Washington University School of Medicine. The successful candidate will serve as an attending cardiac intensivist in a multidisciplinary group of faculty providers, and will also provide consultation in the neonatal and pediatric intensive care units at SLCH. Participation in house staff and fellow education as well as clinical, translational or laboratory-based scholarship and/or administrative leadership will be expected based on the candidate's academic expertise and career goals. Extensive opportunities exist for scholarly collaborations with investigators at the School of Medicine and other departments throughout Washington University. The Saint Louis Children’s Hospital and Washington University Heart Center is a highly ranked pediatric cardiac program including pediatric cardiothoracic surgeons, pediatric cardiologists, pediatric cardiac intensivists and cardiac anesthesiologists who provide clinical care, teach, and perform clinical, translational and basic research. See the following links for details on the Divisions of Pediatric Cardiac Critical Care Medicine, Pediatric Cardiology and the section of Pediatric Cardiothoracic Surgery: &nbsp; Pediatric Cardiac Critical Care Medicine: https://pediatriccriticalcaremedicine.wustl.edu/; Pediatric Cardiology: https://pediatriccardiology.wustl.edu/; and Pediatric Cardiothoracic Surgery: https://pediatriccardiology.wustl.edu/clinical-care/cardiothoracic-surgery/. Our CICU is a state-of-the-art, 16-bed, dedicated unit that provides all forms of cardiovascular intensive care for children and adults with congenital and acquired cardiovascular diseases. Approximately 400 patients are admitted to the CICU per year, with 60% surgical admissions. Patients range in age from premature neonates to adults, and patient complexity and acuity are amongst the highest in the nation. A full range of clinical services is available, including mechanical circulatory support for heart failure patients with ventricular assist devices (Thoratec, Berlin Heart, HeartMate II, HeartWare), the paracorporeal lung assist device system (Novalung and Quadrox oxygenators) and ECMO. We have an internationally renowned heart failure/transplant program and perform approximately 20 heart transplants each year. Critically ill pre- and post- transplant patients are managed in the CICU. Washington University is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to increasing the diversity of its faculty. It welcomes nominations of and applications from women and members of minority groups, as well as others who would bring additional dimensions to the university's research, teaching and clinical missions. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to sex, race, ethnicity, protected veteran, or disability status. Please visit our website at http://hr.wustl.edu to view a summary of benefits. Basic Qualifications The ideal candidate should be board certified/eligible in Pediatric Critical Care Medicine, Pediatric Cardiology, or Pediatric Anesthesia or in a combination of these specialties. Candidates who are board certified/eligible in cardiology or anesthesiology will have completed 1 year of post-fellowship advanced training in cardiac critical care. Candidates who are board certified in Pediatric Critical Care Medicine who have not completed post-fellowship advanced training in cardiac critical care will be considered based on experience. &nbsp; &nbsp; Special Instructions Please contact Dr. Andrew Glatz or Dr. Julie Bubeck Wardenburg with questions related to this posting. Contact Name Andrew Glatz Contact Email glatz@wustl.edu Contact Phone (314)454-4561 Posting Date 1/12/2024 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1010972": {
      "title": "PEM- Faculty member in Pediatric Emergency Medicine, Attending Physician SLCH and CHNW ED- Posting #1010972",
      "description": "Department Department of Pediatrics Division Pediatric Emergency Medicine Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title PEM- Faculty member in Pediatric Emergency Medicine, Attending Physician SLCH and CHNW ED- Posting #1010972 Position Description The Division of Pediatric Emergency Medicine at Washington University School of Medicine is seeking applicants for Pediatric Emergency Medicine physicians (board certified/eligible) or General Pediatricians (board certified/eligible) with experience in pediatric emergency care for work in the Emergency Department at a suburban community hospital (Christian Hospital Northwest Healthcare) and St. Louis Children’s Hospital. Washington University School of Medicine ranks among the top medical schools in the country, and St. Louis Children’s Hospital is also nationally recognized as a leader in pediatric healthcare. The 390-bed pediatric hospital includes a 41 bed pediatric ICU, a 38 bed heart center, and a 125 bed neonatal ICU. The hospital offers comprehensive services in every pediatric and medical subspecialty, serving a diverse patient population in the St. Louis metropolitan area and primary service region covering six states. The Emergency Department provides care for 50,000 children each year, and the hospital has achieved Level 1 Pediatric Trauma Center designation by the American College of Surgeons, as well as the States of Missouri and Illinois. &nbsp;&nbsp; CHNW is a stand-alone emergency department in north St. Louis County. The emergency department sees 11,000 pediatric patients each year. The Department of Pediatrics provides a supportive and intellectually stimulating environment for clinical practice, education and investigation, with unique opportunities for inter- and intra-departmental collaborations. The division supports faculty professional growth and leadership, as well as opportunities to develop expertise in QI, administration, medical education, international health and research. &nbsp; Salary and benefits are competitive. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The St. Louis metropolitan area has approximately 2.5 million people, with diverse, beautiful urban and suburban neighborhoods and moderate cost of living. There are abundant cultural and recreational opportunities. &nbsp; These include a world-class symphony orchestra, professional theater, and college and professional sports. &nbsp; In addition, there are many parks, and trails for hiking, jogging and cycling. &nbsp; Finally, St. Louis region offers outstanding public and private schools and a wide variety of child friendly attractions making it a wonderful place for families. Basic Qualifications Board Certified/Board Eligible Special Instructions Please contact Dr. Kimberly Quayle with any questions related to this posting Contact Name Kimberly Quayle Contact Email quayleks@wustl.edu Contact Phone 314-454-2341 Posting Date 1/8/2024 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1010954": {
      "title": "Pediatric Hospitalist Medicine Faculty Member - Posting #1010954",
      "description": "Department Department of Pediatrics Division Peds Hospitalist Medicine Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Pediatric Hospitalist Medicine Faculty Member - Posting #1010954 Position Description The Division of Pediatric Hospitalist Medicine at Washington University School of Medicine is seeking applicants for faculty positions. Candidates should be board certified/eligible in General Pediatrics and need not be certified in Pediatric Hospital Medicine. &nbsp; PHM faculty are clinician educators at the main academic center and community hospitals within the metro St. Louis area. These hospitals are St. Louis Children’s Hospital, Progress West Hospital, Missouri Baptist Medical Center, Barnes Jewish Hospital, Memorial Hospital-Belleville and Memorial Hospital-East, and any other current or future contracted facilities . We are recruiting for all ranks Instructors/Assistant Professors/ Associate Professors/Professors. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Washington University School of Medicine ranks among the top medical schools in the country, and St. Louis Children’s Hospital is &nbsp; nationally recognized as a leader in pediatric healthcare. The 455-bed pediatric hospital includes a 77 bed pediatric ICU, and a 150 bed neonatal ICU. The hospital offers comprehensive services in every pediatric and medical subspecialty, serving a diverse patient population in the St. Louis metropolitan area and primary service region covering six states. The Pediatric Hospitalist division covers a variety of services at St. Louis Children’s hospital including the general inpatient unit, ambulatory procedure center, after-hours sedation service and the pediatric ambulatory wound service. At our partner hospitals Missouri Baptist Medical Center and Progress West Hospital in Missouri, and Memorial Hospitals in Illinois, our division covers primarily the newborn services and emergency unit. Also at Barnes Jewish Hospital, we cover the well-baby nursery. The Department of Pediatrics provides a supportive and intellectually stimulating environment for clinical practice, education and investigation, with unique opportunities for inter- and intra-departmental collaborations. The division supports faculty professional growth and leadership, as well as opportunities to develop expertise in QI, administration, medical education and research. &nbsp; Salary and benefits are competitive. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The St. Louis metropolitan area has approximately 2.8 million people, with diverse, beautiful urban and suburban neighborhoods and moderate cost of living. There are abundant cultural and recreational opportunities. &nbsp; These include a world-class symphony orchestra, professional theater, and college and professional sports. &nbsp; In addition, there are many parks (including the 1,300 acre Forest Park right across from the hospital) and trails for hiking, jogging and cycling. &nbsp; Finally, St. Louis region offers outstanding public and private schools and a wide variety of child friendly attractions making it a wonderful place for families. &nbsp; Basic Qualifications Board certified/eligible in Pediatrics Additional Qualifications Special Instructions Please visit our faculty jobs site at&nbsp; https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/ &nbsp;to submit your application under posting # For more information, you may: Contact Lisa Moscoso M.D., Ph.D. at&nbsp; lisamoscoso@wustl.edu Visit our webpage at&nbsp; http://pediatrics.wustl.edu/hospitalists Contact Name Lisa Moscoso, M.D., Ph.D. Contact Email lisamoscoso@wustl.edu Posting Date 11/30/2023 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1010906": {
      "title": "Research Instructor in Pediatrics - NBM #1010906",
      "description": "Department Department of Pediatrics Division Pediatrics-Newborn Medicine Track Research Rank Instructor Title Research Instructor in Pediatrics - NBM #1010906 Position Description We are seeking an Instructor level research scientist to join the C. elegans Center for Modeling Human Candidate Disease Variants. The successful candidate will work on projects aimed at new disease gene discovery. Extensive experience in C. elegans genetics, CRISPR/CAS9 editing, neurobiology and cell biology are desirable. &nbsp; Basic Qualifications Ph.D., or M.D., (or foreign equivalent) with minimum 5 years of post-doctoral research experience. Posting Date 7/19/2023 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1010885": {
      "title": "Clinician Educator – Pediatric Nephrology #1010885",
      "description": "Department Department of Pediatrics Division Pediatrics-Nephrology/Inulin Lab Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Clinician Educator – Pediatric Nephrology #1010885 Position Description The Department of Pediatrics at Washington University School of Medicine is seeking a full-time board-eligible or board-certified pediatric nephrologist to join our division of 9 faculty members, 2 advanced practice providers, 4 clinical nurses and multidisciplinary team members. This faculty position, rank commensurate to experience, primarily encompasses 0.8 FTE in clinical care and trainee education. We encourage any type of focused interest for the remaining time in quality improvement, advocacy or clinical research. The division has a thriving clinical program with a wide range of pathology and provides acute and chronic dialysis services, all types of apheresis services (plasma exchange, photopheresis, red and white cell reduction, stem cell collection) and pre- and post-transplant care. We perform our own kidney biopsies. Our school, department and division all have a long-standing track record of outstanding basic and clinical research. We are currently one of 3 NIH-funded Pediatric Centers of Excellence in Nephrology. We have a long-standing ACGME approved Pediatric Nephrology Fellowship program. The division has consistently been listed in the US News and World Report top Pediatric Nephrology programs. Faculty members practice at the attached St. Louis Children’s Hospital, which is part of the very large 28-hospital BJC Healthcare system, as well as in satellite clinics. The WashU Medical Center campus is located next to the 1371-acre Forest Park, one of the largest public parks in the country. The greater St Louis area offers many hidden gems, including world class (and free) museums, numerous theaters, 4 professional sports teams, and plentiful outdoor activities. St Louis has its own hallmark cuisine. The cost of living is very affordable. &nbsp; Basic Qualifications MD Eligible for Missouri and Illinois licensure Board eligible/certified in Pediatric Nephrology &nbsp; Special Instructions Contact Name Carmen Halabi, M.D., Ph.D. Contact Email chalabi@wustl.edu Contact Phone 314-286-1376 Posting Date 5/26/2023 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1010440": {
      "title": "Pediatric Physical Medicine &amp; Rehabilitation specialist #1010440",
      "description": "Department Department of Pediatrics Division Pediatrics Academic Medicine Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Pediatric Physical Medicine &amp; Rehabilitation specialist #1010440 Position Description &nbsp; The candidate will provide Pediatric Physical Medicine &amp; Rehabilitation services in individual and interdisciplinary clinics at St. Louis Children’s Hospital and at other locations where the Department of Pediatrics provides ambulatory services. In addition, the candidate will provide inpatient consultation services for St. Louis Children’s Hospital. &nbsp; Opportunities Opportunity to assist in the continued development and growth of inpatient and outpatient services including: Active inpatient consult service Multiple dedicated Rehab and multidisciplinary clinics The development of dedicated inpatient rehab beds Expanding the practice to Wash U locations outside of SLCH No call or weekend coverage expected Practitioners who potentially have interest in performing procedures in clinic, under sedation and at bedside on inpatient; including but not limited to: Intramuscular botox and chemical alcohol injections Salivary gland toxin injections Intra-articular steroid injections and trigger point injections Currently with access ultrasound, E-stim, and EMG guidance Access to a team/department capable of expanding procedural practices Our team has built a strong presence in the following management and are looking for both practitioners interested in continuing to grow these practices, and expand the team into new areas: Cerebral Palsy management and spasticity management Bracing, Equipment and wheelchairs Pediatric Cancer Rehabilitation Spinal cord injury Sialorrhea management Gait lab and gait analysis Presence in the following Multidisciplinary Clinics: Cerebral Palsy, Neuro-oncology, Skeletal Dysplasia, Spina Bifida Academic program with opportunities for research, resident and student education, and interdisciplinary collaboration Basic Qualifications Board eligible/certified in Pediatric Rehabilitation Medicine or Board eligible/certified in Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation and Pediatrics. Additional Qualifications &nbsp; Special Instructions For additional information or with any questions related to this posting please contact: &nbsp; Dr. Sherea Smith at shereasmith@wustl.edu &nbsp; Posting Date 9/16/2019 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
    },
    "https://facultyopportunities.wustl.edu/Posting/Detail/1010328": {
      "title": "Pediatric Cardiologist (ACHD) #1010328",
      "description": "Department Department of Pediatrics Division Pediatrics-Cardiology/Heart Stat Track Clinician Rank Commensurate w/Experience Title Pediatric Cardiologist (ACHD) #1010328 Position Description The Heart Center at St. Louis Children’s Hospital and the Washington University School of Medicine is recruiting a pediatric or adult cardiologist with an interest in ACHD at the assistant to associate professor level. This individual will join the Division of Pediatric Cardiology and should be board eligible or certified in pediatric or adult cardiology with additional interest in adult congenital heart disease. St Louis Children’s Hospital is one of the premier children’s hospitals in the country. The Heart Center has very active and robust surgical, transplant, interventional, and electrophysiology programs. There are 38 beds within the Heart Center caring for a wide range of very complex cardiac patients. In addition, we have very active outreach clinics and draw patients from throughout the Midwest as well as nationally. Basic Qualifications Board eligible or board certified in Adult Congenital Heart Disease Additional Qualifications Additional training in adult congenital heart disease is mandatory. &nbsp; Interest and performance of clinical research is also desired. Special Instructions Please contact Dr. Andrew Glatz with any questions related to this posting. Contact Name Andrew Glatz Contact Email glatz@wustl.edu Contact Phone (314)454-6095 Posting Date 9/12/2018 End Date No end date EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. Apply Back to List 660 S. Euclid Ave. St. Louis, MO 63110-1010 Equal Employment Opportunity Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity or shared ancestry, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information. &nbsp; As a federal contractor, the university maintains affirmative action programs to implement its equal employment opportunity policy for individuals with disabilities and protected veterans, in accordance with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, as amended, and the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974 (VEVRAA), as amended. © 2026 Washington University in St. Louis Emergency Policies News "
